 explain that more in a second and  thc  you see that red highlighted part there  that  should look pretty similar to you to  laminator right it's because thc is a  terpenophenolic molecule  it has a terpene part to it and a  phenolic part the phenolic part is that  benzene ring with the oh sticking off of  it  so we theorized that thc might make very  similar degradation products  to terpenes and so we had to wait to get  our dna license we got it we started  studying thc but  i want to talk more just briefly about  that isoprene oxidation  because we we looked into the literature  and said well  you know how is ice cream going to react  and what we found is that  uh in the field of atmospheric chemistry  this is actually a pretty well studied  set of reactions uh because these are  isoprene is going to be released in  forests right and so  that ice cream gets up in the atmosphere  and it's going to react with oxygen in  the air it's going to make this  this slew of degradation products which  is very similar  to the degradation products that we  observed from dabbing  terpenes and thc and  this is an interesting implication here  just for another side note  is that there's this uh this study that  was published i'd really recommend  checking this paper out it's open access  everyone wants to check it out  uh done by some well one of the people  on that paper is a collaborator of ours  they looked at the air quality in  cannabis grow rooms outside and in  and so there's a lot of really  interesting interesting details there  one of the major takeaways is that the  high the better the ventilation was the  better the air quality so that's  something you want to keep in mind  because that stuff all those volatile  organics just like the terp beans are  going to actually make ozone and that  might not be good either for  for anyone inhaling it but it also could  degrade  can further oxidize some some components  of  whatever you have in there right so um  yeah so when we got thc in the lab  we we wanted to study this as the best  method that we could  and so one of our collaborators here had  this really interesting  experimental setup called uh automate  adsorption thermal desorption  uh gasoline concrete mass spectrometry  so what this is is  is gcms whoever's if you're familiar  with that already this is a sampling  method for that  that allows you to just basically  collect gases  onto this little tube that you can see  there the atd cartridge that a little  cartridge full of absorbent material  gases get stuck to that  and then a machine is going to  desorb them and they're going to go  right into  uh the gcms essentially and so  that that that atd cartridge can only  handle  gases and so  you need to filter the aerosol of our of  all the particulates and so that  that on this picture here where it says  cfp  holder that's a special specialized  filter called the cambridge filter pad  it's in that holder is going to filter  out the particulates the aerosol right  and so what's interesting then is that  when we see  a chromatogram from dabbing thc  we don't see any thc in here because  this is just  the gases of the aerosol no particulates  so this is just  a way of looking at the degradation  products and so at the  at the lower retention times we see some  c4 to c5  components is acetone there's aline and  a lot of ice brain  unsurprisingly because of how thc is a  turbine  and then we start to see c6 and c7 as we  go up in retention times  we see toluene we see benzene  methacrylan methyl vinyl ketone  cotanaldehyde butanol those are nasty  things you don't want to be inhaling and  there's all these alkanes in there  that toxicologically are not too  relevant but they could be sensitizers  and then at higher retention times you  start to see less and less but  you do see some xylenes uh and  and other c10 components now that's just  from dabbing  thc but remember now terpenes  are going to be heavily present in the  gas phase of the aerosol  and so those large peaks in the green  portion there at around 20 those are  terpenes right so when you dab thc and  terpenes those terpenes are going to get  in the gas phase and you're going to see  them in this chromatogram  and we were able to quantify some of  these degradation products that were  present in the aerosol  we have uh ethacryl and benzene toluene  these are all things we made a  calibration curve for  and you see here at the top you see  uh the thc dab and mock distillate that  mock distillate was  we we purchased uh analytical grade thc  and we added terpenes to it  to make what we call mocht island this  was 90 thc in 10  terpenes and you can see there's a  really significant increase  between from just having thc  to dabbing thc and terpenes a bit large  increase in the degradation products  th terpenes are going to be much more  thermally labeled  which means much more prone to  degradation than just thcs they're  smaller molecules that  they absorb a little bit of energy and  they're just going to start breaking  apart with thcs larger molecules kind of  take more energy to break it apart but  thc still makes and then we also vape  them  we also vape this mixture this ninety  percent thc 10 percent terpenes mixture  put it in a standard like a c cell  cartridge and we vaped it at three  different voltages  and you can see there that as you  increase the  voltage that's an increase in energy  applied to the distillate and  that that's we observed large amounts of  degradation products  so these are pretty uh nasty things here  so i  would encourage everyone to check out  that paper and  to to sort of consider you know what it  is they put in a vape pen we're gonna  have some more results about  some of these components uh for example  mct oil and  other things that people are putting in  there we're gonna study that and  yeah we encourage everyone to really  think about you know what it is they're  they're vaping definitely the more  terpenes you put in there  likely is more degradation products that  are going to be in there because a lot  of people actually say that oh if you  decrease the viscosity of your oil then  that's going to lead to less degradation  well the terpenes  degrade much more easily so it's it's  probably not a good idea  especially and for really anyone that's  that's  that's vaping is going to be exposed to  this so people are more sensitive to  these components  they might want to watch out obviously  uh you know we started talking about  this stuff before  the holy poly outbreak this is the work  that we did  around a year ago and and we published  it right in the middle of the valley  outbreak  and so everything changed after that  but um you know it's still it's still  hold it's it's getting much more  interesting now with all these  developments about the other additives  people are using so that is something  we're going to study  as as time goes on so i want to thank  the national institute of health and the  fda for funding  uh and portland state university also  for funding the drug enforcement agency  helped us with us the security  requirements necessary for us to  legally study schedule one  cannabis in a lab because our lab is  fairly funded so we need to have  we obey through a loss and the pancal  lab  dr wentei luo and kevin porter for  helping us out with the atd  gcms collection methods and my research  assistant  alicia ortiz and obviously my my  principal investigator my pi uh dr  robert strongan  all right thank


